<!--
  IDE plugin descriptor.

  id, name and version are NOT declared here. The IntelliJ Platform Gradle plugin patches them
  in from the `pluginConfiguration` block in build.gradle.kts, and declaring them in both
  places is how the two copies drift. The toolbox sibling hit exactly that: one of two name
  fields was changed, the build stayed green, and the UI still showed the old one.

  The <extensions> block is deliberately absent rather than stubbed. Declaring a toolWindow
  that names a factory class which does not exist yet makes verifyPlugin fail with a
  resolution error, which is a worse first-run experience than a plugin that loads and does
  nothing. Write the factory first, then declare it. CONTEXT.md has the order.

  When the tool window is added, it goes here as:

    <extensions defaultExtensionNs="com.intellij">
      <toolWindow id="XCP-ng" anchor="right" factoryClass="..."/>
    </extensions>
-->
<idea-plugin>
    <vendor url="https://github.com/gounthar/xcpng-intellij-plugin">gounthar</vendor>

    <description><![CDATA[
      See and drive an XCP-ng pool from inside the IDE, through Xen Orchestra.

      Unofficial and unaffiliated with Vates. This is a personal experiment.
    ]]></description>

    <!--
      com.intellij.modules.platform ONLY, and that is the load-bearing choice in this file.

      It is what makes the plugin loadable in every IDE built on the platform rather than in
      IntelliJ IDEA alone, WebStorm included, which matters because the request that prompted
      this repository is a WebStorm request. Adding a dependency on a Java or Kotlin module
      would quietly restrict it to the IDEs that ship one.

      UNVERIFIED: that it does in fact load in WebStorm. Documented behaviour, not observed.
    -->
    <depends>com.intellij.modules.platform</depends>
</idea-plugin>
